      "text": "To find the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\), we start by recalling a useful property of inverses: \\((\\mathbf{A}^2)^{-1} = (\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A})^{-1} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\).\n\nGiven that \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to compute \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the product \\(\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1}\\):\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWe perform the matrix multiplication:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot (-1) & 2 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -1 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-1) & -1 \\cdot 5 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nCalculating each element:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 - 5 & 10 - 15 \\\\ -2 + 3 & -5 + 9 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the inverse of \\(\\mathbf{A}^2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} -1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
